package com.shahaabapps.core.content

/**
 * AZ-305 mock exam 2: sixty new design scenarios, weighted like the skills
 * outline of 17 April 2026 (identity, governance and monitoring 16, data 14,
 * business continuity 11, infrastructure 19). Each sitting draws 50.
 */
internal object Az305Mock3 {
    val quiz: Quiz = Quiz(
        id = "sa-mock-2", title = "AZ-305 mock exam 2", passPercent = 70, timeLimitMinutes = 100, instantFeedback = false, drawCount = 50,
        questions = B.spread(listOf(
            // Identity, governance and monitoring: logging and monitoring
            B.q("sa-m2-1", "sa-monitoring", "Verbose debug fields make up most of the log volume, and nobody queries them. How do you cut the ingestion bill without changing the app?", listOf("Add a transformation to the data collection rule that drops those fields before the data is stored", "Lower the workspace retention to 30 days", "Set a daily cap on the workspace", "Send the logs to a second workspace in another region"), setOf(0), "A data collection rule (DCR) can carry a transformation: a small KQL query that runs as data arrives and can drop rows or columns before they are stored. You pay to ingest only what is kept, and the app does not change.",
                scenario = "Fernhollow Fitness runs the API behind its Android workout app on Azure VMs. The Azure Monitor Agent sends the app's log files to a Log Analytics workspace, and three debug fields that nobody reads make up 70 percent of each record.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Retention changes how long data is kept, but you still pay to ingest every byte.",
                    2 to "A daily cap stops collection once it is reached, so you lose the useful logs as well for the rest of the day.",
                    3 to "A second workspace moves the cost somewhere else. It does not reduce it.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-2", "sa-monitoring", "Alerts must keep firing in the portal during the Sunday maintenance window, but nobody should be paged by email or SMS during it. What do you recommend?", listOf("Disable every alert rule before the window and enable them afterwards", "An alert processing rule that suppresses action groups on a weekly schedule", "Delete the action group and recreate it on Monday", "Raise every alert threshold for the weekend"), setOf(1), "An alert processing rule changes what happens to alerts that have fired. With a schedule, it can suppress the action groups (the email, SMS and other notifications) during a recurring window while the alerts themselves are still recorded.",
                scenario = "Duskwater Bank patches the VMs behind its Android banking app every Sunday from 01:00 to 03:00. The on-call engineers keep being woken by alerts caused by the planned restarts, but the operations lead wants the alerts kept for the record.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Turning rules off by hand each week is easy to forget, and no alerts are recorded while they are off.",
                    2 to "Recreating the action group every week is manual work, and other alerts that use it would go silent too.",
                    3 to "Higher thresholds hide real problems all weekend and still do not stop pages for the restarts.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-3", "sa-monitoring", "The team must know within minutes if the public health endpoint of the API stops answering from several parts of the world. What do you recommend?", listOf("A metric alert on the VM's CPU", "An Azure Service Health alert", "A standard availability test in Application Insights, run from several locations, with an alert", "A diagnostic setting on the API's resource"), setOf(2), "An Application Insights availability test calls a URL on a schedule from several Azure locations around the world and records whether it answered and how fast. An alert on the test fires when calls fail from enough locations.",
                scenario = "Lumen Ferries sells tickets through its Android app, which calls an API hosted on Azure App Service. Last month the API was unreachable for an hour before a customer complained on social media.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "CPU can look normal while the endpoint is down, for example after a bad deployment.",
                    1 to "Service Health reports problems on Azure's side. It does not test your own endpoint.",
                    3 to "Diagnostic settings route logs. They do not call the endpoint to check it answers.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-4", "sa-monitoring", "High-volume container logs are needed only for occasional troubleshooting, a few searches a month. How do you keep them cheaply in the same workspace?", listOf("Keep them in an Analytics table and lower the retention", "Set the table to the Basic logs plan", "Send them to Application Insights instead", "Turn off collection and rely on the container console"), setOf(1), "Log Analytics tables can use different plans. The Basic plan costs much less to ingest, in exchange for simpler queries and a charge per search, which suits large logs that are rarely read.",
                scenario = "Tallyfern's Android shopping app talks to 40 microservices whose container logs reach 200 GB a day. Engineers search them only when a bug is reported, a few times a month, but the ingestion bill is the largest line in the Azure invoice.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "The Analytics plan charges the full ingestion price whatever the retention, and ingestion is where the cost is.",
                    2 to "Application Insights stores data in a workspace too, at the same kind of ingestion price.",
                    3 to "With no collection there is nothing to search when a bug is reported.",
                ),
            ),
            // Identity
            B.q("sa-m2-5", "sa-identity", "Users from two acquired companies, each with its own Active Directory forest that cannot talk to the others, must appear in the parent's Microsoft Entra tenant. What do you recommend with the least on-premises infrastructure?", listOf("Microsoft Entra Connect Sync on one server that reaches every forest", "Microsoft Entra Cloud Sync, with its lightweight agent in each forest", "Invite each user as a B2B guest", "Build a forest trust between all three forests first"), setOf(1), "Cloud Sync is run from the cloud; each forest only needs a small provisioning agent, and it handles forests that are disconnected from each other. Connect Sync needs one server with network reach to every forest.",
                scenario = "Halverson Media bought two smaller studios. Each studio has its own Active Directory forest, and the networks are not joined. All staff need one sign-in for the company's Android editing app, and the IT team has no spare servers.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Connect Sync needs line of sight to every forest from its server, which the separate networks do not give.",
                    2 to "These are employees, not partners. Guest accounts would leave them outside normal staff policies.",
                    3 to "Forest trusts need the networks joined and add work that cloud provisioning does not need.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-6", "sa-identity", "Sign-ins that look risky, such as from an anonymous IP address or with leaked credentials, must be challenged with MFA, and users with leaked passwords must change them. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Entra ID Protection risk signals used in Conditional Access policies", "A named location that blocks every country but one", "Self-service password reset on its own", "Security defaults"), setOf(0), "Microsoft Entra ID Protection scores each sign-in and each user for risk. Conditional Access policies can then require MFA for risky sign-ins and a secure password change for risky users. These risk-based policies need Entra ID P2.",
                scenario = "Kingsbarrow Insurance's claims staff use an Android app from homes and hotels. The security team saw logins from anonymising networks and wants the system to react to risk automatically, not to block whole countries.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Blocking by country is crude and misses risky sign-ins from the allowed country.",
                    2 to "SSPR lets users reset passwords, but nothing tells them when to, or challenges a risky sign-in.",
                    3 to "Security defaults turn on basic MFA for everyone, without any risk-based decisions.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-7", "sa-identity", "The GitHub Actions workflow that deploys the Android app's backend must sign in to Azure without any stored secret or certificate. What do you recommend?", listOf("A service principal with a client secret saved as a GitHub secret", "A personal access token from an administrator", "A storage account key saved in the repository", "Workload identity federation: a federated credential that trusts the repository's GitHub tokens"), setOf(3), "Workload identity federation lets an app registration or user-assigned managed identity trust tokens issued by GitHub for a given repository and branch. The workflow swaps that short-lived token for an Entra token, so there is no secret to store or rotate.",
                scenario = "Gullwing Travel deploys its booking API with GitHub Actions. A client secret leaked from an old pipeline last year, and the security team now forbids long-lived credentials in CI/CD.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A client secret is exactly the long-lived credential the team has banned.",
                    1 to "A personal token ties deployments to one person and is still a stored secret.",
                    2 to "A storage key is a secret with full access, and keeping it in a repository is worse still.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-8", "sa-identity", "Administrators must use a sign-in method that cannot be phished, while other staff may keep using the Authenticator app. What do you configure? Select two.", listOf("Register FIDO2 security keys or passkeys for the administrators", "A Conditional Access policy for admin roles that requires the phishing-resistant MFA authentication strength", "SMS one-time codes for administrators", "A longer password policy for administrators"), setOf(0, 1), "Phishing-resistant methods, such as FIDO2 keys and passkeys, are bound to the real sign-in site, so a fake page cannot replay them. An authentication strength in Conditional Access requires those methods for the chosen users while others keep their usual MFA.",
                scenario = "After a phishing attack copied an administrator's MFA code at Ostrava Robotics, the CISO wants admins to use methods a fake login page cannot capture. Staff who only use the Android timesheet app should not be affected.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "SMS codes can be typed into a fake page and replayed, which is the attack that just happened.",
                    3 to "Longer passwords do nothing against a page that captures the password and the code together.",
                ),
            ),
            // Authorization and secrets
            B.q("sa-m2-9", "sa-authorization", "Each partner's integration must read only the blobs tagged with its own partner name, all in one container, with no new container per partner. What do you recommend?", listOf("A Storage Blob Data Reader assignment with a condition on the blob index tag", "Storage Blob Data Reader on the whole container", "A separate storage account for each partner", "An account SAS shared with each partner"), setOf(0), "Azure attribute-based access control (ABAC) adds conditions to a role assignment. A condition can allow reads only of blobs whose index tag, a key-value label on the blob, matches the partner, so one container can safely hold everyone's files.",
                scenario = "Quayline Logistics drops delivery reports for 30 courier partners into one container, tagged with the partner's name. Each partner reads its reports with a managed identity, and must never see another partner's files.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Reader on the whole container lets every partner read every other partner's reports.",
                    2 to "Thirty storage accounts is the extra structure the company wants to avoid.",
                    3 to "An account SAS opens the whole account and is a secret that is hard to take back.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-10", "sa-authorization", "Office workers must reach on-premises file shares and a remote desktop server from home, with Conditional Access checks and no VPN. The apps are not web apps. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Entra application proxy", "Microsoft Entra Private Access, part of Global Secure Access", "Open the SMB and RDP ports to the internet", "Azure Bastion in a hub VNet"), setOf(1), "Microsoft Entra Private Access gives secure access to private apps of any TCP or UDP kind, such as SMB file shares and RDP, through a client on the device and a connector on-premises. Every connection passes Conditional Access, and no VPN is needed.",
                scenario = "Brightwell Accountants has file servers and a remote desktop host in its office. Staff working from home also use the firm's Android expense app, which already signs in with Microsoft Entra ID. The old VPN appliance is out of support.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Application proxy publishes HTTPS endpoints. It reaches remote desktops only through a Remote Desktop Services deployment, with RD Web and RD Gateway published together, and it cannot carry SMB file share traffic at all.",
                    2 to "Opening SMB and RDP to the internet invites attacks and offers no Conditional Access.",
                    3 to "Bastion reaches Azure VMs, not file shares and servers in the office.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-11", "sa-authorization", "The web app's TLS certificate must renew itself before it expires, with no one copying files around, and the App Service app must pick up the new one. What do you recommend?", listOf("Store the certificate on a file share and remind the team each year", "Upload the PFX file to App Service by hand each year", "Issue the certificate in Key Vault through an integrated certificate authority with auto-renewal, and import it into App Service from Key Vault", "Use a self-signed certificate that never expires"), setOf(2), "Key Vault can request and renew certificates automatically from its integrated certificate authorities, DigiCert and GlobalSign. An App Service certificate imported from Key Vault is synchronised when the vault holds a new version.",
                scenario = "The website that hosts Pinecrest Clinic's Android app download page went offline last year because its certificate expired over a holiday. The clinic wants renewals to happen without anyone remembering.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A reminder depends on people, which is what failed last year.",
                    1 to "Manual uploads each year are the same risk under another name.",
                    3 to "Browsers and phones do not trust self-signed certificates on a public site.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-12", "sa-authorization", "An app must read secrets from one vault, and the team must not be able to grant itself access to keys or change vault settings. Which permission model and role do you recommend?", listOf("The Azure RBAC permission model, with Key Vault Secrets User for the app's managed identity", "Vault access policies granting all secret permissions", "The Key Vault Contributor role for the app", "The Key Vault Administrator role for the app"), setOf(0), "Microsoft recommends the Azure RBAC permission model for Key Vault. Key Vault Secrets User only reads secret values, and it can be scoped to the vault or a single secret. Changing who has access stays with people who hold role-assignment rights.",
                scenario = "Saffron Kitchens' order API, which serves its Android delivery app, reads a database connection string from Key Vault. The auditors found that the app's identity could list and change every key in the vault.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "All secret permissions include set and delete, far more than reading, and anyone with Key Vault Contributor can change access policies.",
                    2 to "Key Vault Contributor manages the vault resource, including its access policies, but does not read secret values through RBAC.",
                    3 to "Administrator can do everything with keys, secrets and certificates.",
                ),
            ),
            // Governance
            B.q("sa-m2-13", "sa-governance", "Nobody may delete resources tagged Critical=true, but the resources must stay fully changeable. What do you recommend so that it applies across every subscription automatically?", listOf("A CanNotDelete lock on every critical resource, added by hand", "A ReadOnly lock on each resource group", "An RBAC role without delete rights for everyone", "An Azure Policy with the denyAction effect on delete for resources tagged Critical=true, assigned at the top management group"), setOf(3), "The denyAction policy effect blocks a named action, today the delete action, on resources that match the policy rule. Assigned at a management group, it covers every current and future subscription below it with no per-resource work.",
                scenario = "Marrowfield Energy tags the databases and storage behind its Android meter-reading app as Critical=true. Twice this year an engineer deleted one while tidying a resource group. Teams still need to change settings freely.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Locks added by hand get missed on new resources, which is the gap that caused the deletions.",
                    1 to "ReadOnly stops every change, which breaks the requirement to keep the resources changeable.",
                    2 to "Removing delete rights from everyone also blocks deleting things that should be deleted.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-14", "sa-governance", "One team's legacy VM breaks the 'no public IP' policy while it is replaced over the next three months. How should it be handled?", listOf("Remove the policy assignment until the VM is replaced", "A policy exemption for that VM, category Waiver, with an expiry date three months away", "Move the VM to a subscription with no policies", "Change the policy effect to disabled"), setOf(1), "A policy exemption takes one resource or scope out of an assignment's evaluation, records why (Waiver or Mitigated) and can expire on a date. The rest of the estate stays under the policy.",
                scenario = "At Corrigan Health the policy that denies public IP addresses blocks nothing new, but an old reporting VM still needs one until its replacement for the Android app's admin portal goes live in three months.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Removing the assignment exposes every other resource for three months.",
                    2 to "Moving resources to escape governance creates an ungoverned subscription.",
                    3 to "Disabled switches the policy off for everyone, not just for one VM.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-15", "sa-governance", "Developers want subscriptions to experiment in, with looser rules and no connection to the company network, kept apart from production. Where in the management group hierarchy should they go?", listOf("Under the Corp landing zone management group", "Under the Platform management group", "In a Sandbox management group with its own lighter policies", "Directly under the tenant root group with no policies"), setOf(2), "The Azure landing zone design has a Sandbox management group for experiments. Its policies are lighter, but its subscriptions are not connected to the corporate network, so mistakes there cannot reach production.",
                scenario = "Rivenhall Games wants its Android developers to try new Azure services quickly. The platform team uses the Azure landing zone hierarchy, with Platform, Landing zones (Corp and Online) and Decommissioned management groups.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Corp landing zones are connected to the company network and get the strictest policies.",
                    1 to "Platform holds shared services such as identity, management and connectivity, not experiments.",
                    3 to "The root group with no policies leaves the subscriptions ungoverned, and policies at the root would apply to everything anyway.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-16", "sa-governance", "When someone joins, their access must be set up on day one, and when they leave, their access must be removed and their manager told, automatically. What do you recommend?", listOf("Lifecycle workflows in Microsoft Entra ID Governance", "Quarterly access reviews", "A Conditional Access policy", "Privileged Identity Management"), setOf(0), "Lifecycle workflows automate joiner, mover and leaver tasks, such as adding groups, sending welcome emails and removing access on the last day, triggered by attributes like the hire or leave date.",
                scenario = "Hallam Logistics hires 50 seasonal drivers each autumn. They use the company's Android route app and must have access from day one, and lose it on their last day without the IT team doing it by hand.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Reviews check access every few months, so leavers keep access until the next review.",
                    2 to "Conditional Access decides how people sign in. It does not grant or remove access over time.",
                    3 to "PIM manages privileged roles, not everyday access for joiners and leavers.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data: relational
            B.q("sa-m2-17", "sa-relational", "All tenants share one Azure SQL database, and the app must never return one tenant's rows to another, even if a query forgets a WHERE clause. What do you recommend?", listOf("Row-level security with a security policy that filters on the tenant ID", "Dynamic data masking on the tenant ID column", "A separate login for each table", "Always Encrypted on every column"), setOf(0), "Row-level security adds a predicate the database applies to every query, so each session sees only rows for its own tenant, whatever the query says. The filter lives in the database rather than in each query.",
                scenario = "Wicklow Tutors' Android app serves 300 schools from one Azure SQL database with a TenantId column on every table. A bug in one query showed a school another school's timetables.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Masking hides values on screen, but the other tenant's rows still come back.",
                    2 to "Per-table logins do not separate rows that sit in the same table.",
                    3 to "Always Encrypted protects values from admins. It does not decide which rows a tenant sees.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-18", "sa-relational", "Analysts run heavy reports on a Hyperscale database, and they must not slow the app or each other, with compute sized for the reports. What do you recommend?", listOf("Scale the primary up to more vCores", "A named replica of the Hyperscale database, with its own compute size, for the analysts", "Copy the database each night with a database copy", "Move the reports to the high availability replica"), setOf(1), "A Hyperscale named replica is a read-only copy that shares the same storage, so it is ready in minutes and costs no extra storage. It has its own compute size and its own connection, so report load stays away from the primary.",
                scenario = "Copperline Telecom's usage database for its Android account app is on Azure SQL Hyperscale. The analytics team's reports run for hours and need more memory than the app, and they must see data minutes old at most.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A bigger primary still shares its resources between the app and the reports.",
                    2 to "A nightly copy of a large database takes time and storage, and the data would be up to a day old.",
                    3 to "High availability replicas are sized like the primary and are there for failover, not for a separate reporting size.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-19", "sa-relational", "The regulator requires that the bank controls the encryption key for its database at rest and can make the data unreadable at once by revoking it. What do you recommend?", listOf("Transparent data encryption with a service-managed key", "Transparent data encryption with a customer-managed key in Azure Key Vault", "Dynamic data masking", "Backups encrypted with a password"), setOf(1), "Transparent data encryption (TDE) encrypts the database files at rest. With a customer-managed key, the key lives in your Key Vault; revoke the database's access to it and the database becomes inaccessible.",
                scenario = "Northgate Credit Union's Android banking app stores accounts in Azure SQL Database. Its regulator insists that the union owns the key that protects data at rest and can cut off access in an emergency.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A service-managed key is held by Microsoft, so the union cannot revoke it.",
                    2 to "Masking changes what users see in results. It does not encrypt anything at rest.",
                    3 to "Encrypting only backups leaves the live database outside the union's control.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-20", "sa-relational", "An app built on PostgreSQL uses the PostGIS extension for map queries and must move to a managed service with as few changes as possible. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure SQL Database", "Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL", "SQL Server on an Azure VM", "Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server"), setOf(3), "Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server is managed PostgreSQL and supports common extensions such as PostGIS, the spatial extension for map data, so the app and its SQL keep working.",
                scenario = "Trailmark's Android hiking app stores routes in PostgreSQL on a VM the team patches by hand. Most queries use PostGIS functions to find trails near a point.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Azure SQL is a different engine, so every PostGIS query would need rewriting.",
                    1 to "Cosmos DB is a NoSQL database, which means redesigning the data and queries.",
                    2 to "A VM keeps the patching work the team wants to drop, and SQL Server still is not PostgreSQL.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-21", "sa-relational", "Which needs push a SQL Server workload onto SQL Server on an Azure VM rather than Azure SQL Managed Instance? Select two.", listOf("SQL Server Reporting Services installed alongside the database engine", "SQL Server Agent jobs", "Cross-database queries", "FILESTREAM data stored on the server's file system"), setOf(0, 3), "Managed Instance supports most of SQL Server, including Agent jobs and cross-database queries, but not FILESTREAM or running Reporting Services on the instance. When you need those, or the operating system itself, SQL Server on a VM is the fit.",
                scenario = "Harbourside Museums' collection database stores scanned images with FILESTREAM, and the curators' reports run on SQL Server Reporting Services on the same server. The museum's Android guide app reads from it.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Managed Instance runs SQL Server Agent jobs.",
                    2 to "Managed Instance supports queries across databases on the same instance.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data: semi-structured and unstructured
            B.q("sa-m2-22", "sa-nonrelational", "Most tenants are small, but a few large tenants each produce more than 20 GB of data and heavy traffic, so one partition key value per tenant would be too hot. What do you recommend in Azure Cosmos DB?", listOf("A partition key on a random number per item", "A hierarchical partition key: tenant ID, then user ID", "A partition key on the creation date", "One container per tenant"), setOf(1), "Hierarchical partition keys let you partition on up to three levels, such as tenant and then user. A large tenant spreads over many physical partitions, and queries for one tenant still go only to that tenant's partitions.",
                scenario = "Beaconfield builds a white-label Android loyalty app used by 2,000 shops. A logical partition in Cosmos DB, all items with the same key, is limited to 20 GB, and three big supermarket chains are already near it.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A random key spreads writes but makes every per-tenant query fan out to all partitions.",
                    2 to "A date key sends all of today's writes to one hot partition.",
                    3 to "Thousands of containers is hard to run and wastes throughput on small tenants.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-23", "sa-nonrelational", "A new app has a few hundred requests an hour with long quiet spells, runs in one region, and cost must follow actual use. Which Cosmos DB capacity mode fits?", listOf("Provisioned throughput, manual", "Autoscale provisioned throughput", "Serverless", "Provisioned throughput with a reserved capacity purchase"), setOf(2), "Serverless charges only for the request units, Cosmos DB's measure of work done, that your operations use, with no minimum throughput. It suits light, bursty traffic in a single region, which is what it supports.",
                scenario = "Mossgate Library is launching an Android app for reserving study rooms. Usage is light and uneven, the app will run only in one region, and the library pays for what it uses.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Manual provisioned throughput bills for the capacity reserved every hour, busy or not.",
                    1 to "Autoscale still bills at least 10 percent of its maximum each hour.",
                    3 to "Reserved capacity discounts a steady commitment, the opposite of light and uneven use.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-24", "sa-nonrelational", "Partners can only send files over SFTP, and the files must land in Blob Storage with no servers to run. What do you recommend?", listOf("A Linux VM running an SFTP server that copies files to Blob Storage", "SFTP support enabled on a storage account with a hierarchical namespace, with a local user per partner", "Azure Files over SMB", "An FTP site on App Service"), setOf(1), "Blob Storage can accept SFTP connections directly. It needs a hierarchical namespace (the Data Lake Storage feature that adds real folders), and each partner signs in as a local user with its own home folder and permissions.",
                scenario = "Kelpie Freight receives delivery manifests from 15 hauliers whose systems only speak SFTP. The manifests feed the Android driver app, and the team does not want to patch a file server.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A VM is the server the team does not want to run.",
                    2 to "Azure Files speaks SMB and NFS, not SFTP.",
                    3 to "FTP on App Service is meant for deploying code, and plain FTP is not SFTP.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-25", "sa-nonrelational", "Session documents must disappear by themselves 24 hours after the last update, without a clean-up job. What do you configure in Azure Cosmos DB?", listOf("A lifecycle management policy", "Time to live (TTL) on the container, set to 86,400 seconds", "A stored procedure scheduled every hour", "Soft delete on the container"), setOf(1), "Time to live tells Cosmos DB to delete items a set number of seconds after they were last written, using spare throughput. 86,400 seconds is 24 hours, and nothing has to be scheduled.",
                scenario = "Glasshouse Cinemas' Android booking app keeps a seat-hold document for each customer's session in Cosmos DB. Old holds pile up and slow queries, and the team does not want a clean-up job to maintain.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Lifecycle management policies are a Blob Storage feature, not Cosmos DB.",
                    2 to "Cosmos DB has no scheduler for stored procedures, and this is the clean-up job they want to avoid.",
                    3 to "Soft delete keeps deleted things for recovery. It does not delete old items.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-26", "sa-nonrelational", "Millions of small sensor files are written and read with consistently low latency, and the per-transaction cost matters more than the per-gigabyte cost. Which storage do you recommend?", listOf("A premium block blob storage account", "A standard general-purpose v2 account in the hot tier", "A standard account in the cool tier", "Azure NetApp Files"), setOf(0), "Premium block blob accounts keep data on SSDs, giving low and steady latency, and charge less per transaction but more per gigabyte. That suits workloads with many small, frequent reads and writes.",
                scenario = "Wavecrest Marine's buoys upload a small reading every few seconds, and its Android app shows live charts from them. Storage costs are mostly transactions, and slow writes delay the charts.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard hot storage is on hard disks with higher latency and higher transaction prices.",
                    2 to "Cool is for data read rarely, with even higher transaction prices.",
                    3 to "NetApp Files is a file service for NFS and SMB workloads, more than this needs.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data integration
            B.q("sa-m2-27", "sa-data-integration", "Analysts need the operational Azure SQL data in the company's Microsoft Fabric lakehouse within minutes, without building or running ETL pipelines. What do you recommend?", listOf("A nightly Data Factory copy pipeline", "Export a BACPAC file every hour", "Mirroring of the Azure SQL database into Microsoft Fabric", "Transactional replication to a VM"), setOf(2), "Fabric mirroring keeps a near real-time copy of an Azure SQL database in OneLake, Fabric's single data lake, in Delta format. There is no pipeline to build, and analysts can query it at once.",
                scenario = "Pebblebrook Retail's Android shopping app writes orders to Azure SQL Database. The data team already uses Microsoft Fabric for Power BI reports and wants today's orders in them, not yesterday's.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A nightly pipeline is ETL to build and run, and the data is up to a day old.",
                    1 to "BACPAC exports are for moving whole databases, are slow, and are not queryable in a lake.",
                    3 to "Replicating to a VM adds a server and still does not put the data in Fabric.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m2-28", "sa-data-integration", "A pipeline must process each hour of data once, rerun past hours after a fix, and wait until the previous hour succeeded. Which Data Factory trigger do you use?", listOf("A schedule trigger", "A tumbling window trigger", "A storage event trigger", "A manual run each hour"), setOf(1), "A tumbling window trigger fires for fixed, back-to-back time windows and passes each window's start and end to the pipeline. It can backfill past windows, retry them, and depend on the previous window.",
                scenario = "Riverstone Energy aggregates readings from its Android meter app every hour. After a bug fix, the team must rerun last week's hours, and each hour depends on the one before.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A schedule trigger just fires at times. It does not track windows, backfill them or chain them.",
